# Claim 1

A process for the selective hydrogenation of highly unsaturated hydrocarbons present in a hydrocarbon feed stream, said process comprising:

(a) introducing a feed stream comprising at least one C₂–C₅ hydrocarbon fraction containing highly unsaturated hydrocarbon impurities selected from the group consisting of acetylene, methylacetylene, propadiene, and mixtures thereof, together with a hydrogen-containing gas, into a reaction zone;

(b) contacting said feed stream, within said reaction zone, with a supported hydrogenation catalyst comprising a Group VIII metal selected from palladium, platinum, or mixtures thereof, deposited on a porous inorganic support, wherein said catalyst exhibits a selectivity ratio S, defined as:

  S = (moles of highly unsaturated hydrocarbons converted to mono-olefins) / (total moles of highly unsaturated hydrocarbons converted)

  such that S is greater than or equal to 0.85, and wherein said catalyst limits the formation of saturated hydrocarbon by-products to less than 10 mol% of the total converted highly unsaturated hydrocarbons;

(c) maintaining, within said reaction zone, a flow index F defined by the equation:

  F = (Q_H2 / Q_HC) × (P/P₀) × (T₀/T) × (1/√(ρ_g/ρ₀))

  wherein:
   Q_H2 = volumetric flow rate of hydrogen-containing gas (Nm³/h);
   Q_HC = volumetric flow rate of hydrocarbon feed (Nm³/h);
   P = operating pressure of the reaction zone (bar absolute);
   P₀ = reference pressure (1 bar absolute);
   T = operating temperature of the reaction zone (Kelvin);
   T₀ = reference temperature (298 K);
   ρ_g = density of the gas mixture at operating conditions;
   ρ₀ = reference density of the gas mixture at standard conditions;

  wherein said flow index F is maintained within the range of 1.05 to 3.50, thereby ensuring a hydrogen-to-highly-unsaturated-hydrocarbon molar ratio sufficient to promote selective hydrogenation while suppressing over-hydrogenation to saturated species;

(d) operating said reaction zone at a temperature between 40°C and 120°C and a pressure between 5 and 30 bar absolute, such that the highly unsaturated hydrocarbon content of the feed stream is reduced to less than 5 ppm by weight while maintaining a mono-olefin yield increase of at least 90% of the theoretical maximum based on the stoichiometric hydrogenation of the highly unsaturated hydrocarbons present;

(e) withdrawing from said reaction zone a product stream having a reduced concentration of highly unsaturated hydrocarbons and an increased concentration of the corresponding mono-olefins relative to said feed stream.

---

## Claim 2 (dependent)

The process of claim 1, wherein the catalyst further comprises a selectivity-modifying promoter selected from silver, gold, or a Group IA/IIA metal, present in an amount of 0.01 to 5 wt% based on the total catalyst weight, wherein said promoter increases the selectivity ratio S to a value greater than or equal to 0.90.

## Claim 3 (dependent)

The process of claim 1, wherein the flow index F is maintained within the range of 1.20 to 2.50 by dynamic adjustment of the hydrogen-containing gas flow rate Q_H2 in response to an on-line measurement of highly unsaturated hydrocarbon concentration at the inlet of the reaction zone.

## Claim 4 (dependent)

The process of claim 1, wherein the reaction zone comprises at least two catalyst beds arranged in series, with an intermediate cooling stage disposed between said beds, and wherein the flow index F is independently maintained within the range set forth in claim 1 in each of said catalyst beds.

## Claim 5 (dependent)

The process of claim 1, wherein the highly unsaturated hydrocarbon is acetylene, the corresponding mono-olefin is ethylene, and the product stream contains less than 1 ppm by weight of residual acetylene.
